"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[52827],{52827:function(n){n.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n## Fail2ban: Installation, Configuration, and Manual Ban/Unban\\n\\n<mark>Fail2ban</mark> is a security tool that monitors log files and, upon detecting malicious access patterns such as repeated login failures or port scans, automatically adds the offending IP to ```iptables``` or ```nftables``` firewall rules to block it.\\n\\nIt is most commonly used to protect against SSH brute-force attacks.\\n\\n### 1. Install Fail2ban\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Install Fail2ban\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo apt update\\n$ sudo apt install fail2ban -y\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### 2. Configure jail.local\\nSince <mark>jail.conf</mark> can be overwritten during updates, always create a separate <mark>jail.local</mark> for your configuration.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Create jail.local from default config\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo cp /etc/fail2ban/jail.conf /etc/fail2ban/jail.local\\n$ sudo vi /etc/fail2ban/jail.local\\n```\\n\\nSpecify common settings in the `[DEFAULT]` section, and SSH-specific settings in the `[sshd]` section.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"/etc/fail2ban/jail.local\\"\\n}\\n---\\n[DEFAULT]\\n# Ban duration in seconds (-1 for permanent ban)\\nbantime  = 3600\\n# Ban if maxretry is exceeded within this time window (seconds)\\nfindtime = 600\\n# Number of failures allowed before ban\\nmaxretry = 5\\n# Whitelisted IPs (excluded from banning)\\nignoreip = 127.0.0.1/8 ::1\\n\\n[sshd]\\nenabled = true\\nport    = ssh\\nlogpath = %(sshd_log)s\\nbackend = %(sshd_backend)s\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### 3. Start & Enable Service\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Start and enable Fail2ban service\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo systemctl enable --now fail2ban\\n$ sudo systemctl status fail2ban\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### 4. Check Status\\nCheck the full list of jails and the ban status of each.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Check all jail status\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo fail2ban-client status\\nStatus\\n|- Number of jail:      1\\n`- Jail list:           sshd\\n```\\n\\nCheck the detailed status of a specific jail (including the list of banned IPs).\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Check sshd jail status\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo fail2ban-client status sshd\\nStatus for the jail: sshd\\n|- Filter\\n|  |- Currently failed: 2\\n|  |- Total failed:     47\\n|  `- File list:        /var/log/auth.log\\n`- Actions\\n   |- Currently banned: 1\\n   |- Total banned:     3\\n//[!code highlight]\\n   `- Banned IP list:   203.0.113.42\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### 5. Manual Ban\\nManually ban a specific IP immediately.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Manually ban an IP\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo fail2ban-client set sshd banip 203.0.113.42\\n1\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### 6. Manual Unban\\nManually unban a banned IP.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Manually unban an IP\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo fail2ban-client set sshd unbanip 203.0.113.42\\n1\\n```\\n```adcard\\n```\\n### 7. Reload Configuration\\nAfter modifying `jail.local`, reload the configuration without restarting the service.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Reload Fail2ban configuration\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo fail2ban-client reload\\n```\\n\\n### Command Summary\\n\\n| Command | Description |\\n|---|---|\\n| `fail2ban-client status` | Check full jail list |\\n| `fail2ban-client status sshd` | Check specific jail status and banned IP list |\\n| `fail2ban-client set sshd banip <IP>` | Manually ban an IP |\\n| `fail2ban-client set sshd unbanip <IP>` | Unban an IP |\\n| `fail2ban-client reload` | Reload configuration |\\n| `systemctl restart fail2ban` | Restart the service |\\n"}')}}]);